<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Muxr's window theme — the **day** configuration.

  Muxr is a permanently dark app: `MuxrApp`'s `setup` installs the Glyph dark
  design system unconditionally, and there is no light variant of the design to
  switch to. So this theme must not vary with the device's day/night setting,
  which is exactly what the `values-night/` sibling of this file exists to
  guarantee — see it for the whole story.

  Everything lives in `Theme.Muxr.Base` below and both leaves are empty
  children of it. That is deliberate: it is what makes a future divergence
  between day and night a *deliberate* act rather than a one-line accident.

  ## What this theme changes, and what it deliberately does not

  Without this theme the activity runs on the bare platform
  `@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar`, which leaves `windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds`
  at `false`: the **system** then draws the status/navigation bar backgrounds,
  opaquely and in its own black, over an app that has already laid itself out
  edge-to-edge. The result is a pitch-black bar sitting on top of the app's own
  chrome instead of the app's tone showing through.

  So: the window takes ownership of those backgrounds and paints them
  transparent. Nothing else moves.

  **Inset semantics are untouched.** The Frust Android embedding
  (`FrustActivity.onCreate`) calls `WindowCompat.setDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false)`
  and `FrustSurfaceView`'s `OnApplyWindowInsetsListener` reports the resulting
  system-bar/cutout/IME occlusion into the framework, where the app's
  `safe_area`s and `topbar_clearance` consume it. `windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds`
  changes **who paints the bar background**, not what the window reports as
  insets and not how content is fitted — so every one of those consumers sees
  exactly what it saw before. This theme sets no `windowFullscreen`, no
  `windowTranslucentStatus`/`windowTranslucentNavigation` (which *would* alter
  fitting), and no `fitsSystemWindows`.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
      The shared base every configuration below inherits. Parented on the same
      platform theme the manifest used to name directly, so nothing about the
      window's decor (no title bar, not fullscreen — the embedding's docs are
      explicit that `SystemUiMode.edgeToEdge` wants bars *visible*) changes.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Muxr.Base" parent="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar">

        <!--
          Let the *window* draw the system-bar backgrounds instead of the
          system. Without this the two colors below are ignored outright and
          the platform paints its own opaque bars over the app.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>

        <!--
          …and draw them as nothing at all, so what shows behind the status bar
          is the app's own painted tone: the top bar's surface, which already
          runs the full inset-extended height (`ui::topbar`'s "Top-inset
          consumption"), or a screen's own backdrop where there is no bar.
        -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>

        <!--
          The navigation bar has the identical defect and takes the identical
          fix. Safe here because the app is permanently dark AND already
          reserves the gesture-nav inset (`ui::sheet`'s bottom safe area, every
          page's `safe_area`), so nothing lands under the bar — only the app's
          background tone does.
        -->
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>

        <!--
          Force LIGHT status-bar icons in *every* configuration.

          `windowLightStatusBar=true` means "the bar background is light, so
          draw dark icons" — which is what a device in light mode would
          otherwise pick, giving black icons on this app's black chrome. Muxr
          is dark in both configurations, so this is `false` here and `false`
          in `values-night/` too.

          KNOWN LIMITATION, and the reason the `values-night/` file exists as
          documentation as much as configuration: this attribute only seeds the
          window's *initial* appearance. `FrustSurfaceView.updateSystemBarsAppearance`
          re-derives both bar-icon appearances from the **device's**
          `Configuration.uiMode` at surface-create and on every configuration
          change, through `WindowInsetsControllerCompat`, and a runtime call
          overrides a theme attribute. An app whose design system is not the
          device's brightness therefore cannot hold this from `res/` alone —
          filed against the framework rather than worked around here (the fix
          is for that call to follow the *installed theme's* brightness, which
          the shell already knows).
        -->
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
    </style>

    <!-- The theme the manifest applies. Empty by design — see the file docs. -->
    <style name="Theme.Muxr" parent="Theme.Muxr.Base" />

</resources>
